@chapter AGI Frontend Language
@cindex AGI frontend language

This chapter describes the AGI frontend language as implemented in the EightBol compiler.
The language is defined by its lexer (@file{agi-lexer.lisp}) and parser (@file{agi-parser.lisp}).
Only the constructs defined in these files are part of the language; any other AGI-like
constructs are not supported by this frontend.

@section Lexical Elements

The AGI frontend lexer tokenizes input into the following categories:

@table @asis
@item Keywords
Reserved words that have special meaning in the language.
@item Operators
Symbols representing arithmetic, logical, and comparison operations.
@item Delimiters
Punctuation used for grouping and separation.
@item Literals
Numeric and string constants.
@item Identifiers
User-defined names for variables and routines.
@end table

@subsection Keywords

The following keywords are recognized (case-insensitive):
@table @asis
@item IF
Starts a conditional statement.
@item ELSE
Specifies the false branch of an IF statement.
@item THEN
Separates the condition from the true branch in an IF statement.
@item GOSUB
Calls a subroutine.
@item GOTO
Jumps to a labeled statement.
@item NEWROOM
(Reserved for future use; not implemented in the parser.)
@item LOADLOGICS
(Reserved for future use; not implemented in the parser.)
@item RETURN
Returns from a subroutine.
@item QUIT
(Reserved for future use; not implemented in the parser.)
@item ASSIGN
(Reserved for future use; not implemented in the parser. Assignment is done via the @code{=} operator.)
@item INCREMENT
Increments a variable by 1.
@item DECREMENT
Decrements a variable by 1.
@item SET
Sets a variable to 1.
@item RESET
Sets a variable to 0.
@item SAID
(Reserved for future use; not implemented in the parser.)
@item TEST
(Reserved for future use; not implemented in the parser.)
@item POSN
(Reserved for future use; not implemented in the parser.)
@item CONTROLLER
(Reserved for future use; not implemented in the parser.)
@item HAVEKEY
(Reserved for future use; not implemented in the parser.)
@item AND
Logical conjunction operator.
@item OR
Logical disjunction operator.
@end table

@subsection Operators

The following operators are supported:
@table @asis
@item @code{+}
Addition.
@item @code{-}
Subtraction.
@item @code{*}
Multiplication.
@item @code{/}
Division.
@item @code{=}
Assignment and equality comparison.
@item @code{<}
Less than comparison.
@item @code{>}
Greater than comparison.
@item @code{<=}
Less than or equal comparison.
@item @code{>=}
Greater than or equal comparison.
@item @code{<>}
Not equal comparison.
@item @code{<<}
Left bit shift.
@item @code{>>}
Right bit shift.
@item @code{.}
(Reserved for future use; not implemented in the parser.)
@end table

@subsection Delimiters

The following delimiters are used:
@table @asis
@item @code{(}
Left parenthesis for grouping expressions.
@item @code{)}
Right parenthesis for grouping expressions.
@item @code{,}
Comma for separating items (reserved for future use).
@item @code{:}
Colon for separating items (reserved for future use).
@end table

@subsection Literals

@table @asis
@item Numeric literals
Integer constants in one of the following formats:
@table @asis
@item Decimal
Bare digits (@code{42}, @code{0}, @code{123}) or @code{d'@var{digits}'} format.
Examples: @code{100}, @code{d'255'}
@item Hexadecimal
@code{x'@var{hex-digits}'} format with optional commas for readability.
Examples: @code{x'FF'}, @code{x'1A,2B'}
@item Octal
@code{o'@var{octal-digits}'} format.
Examples: @code{o'777'}, @code{o'42'}
@item Binary
@code{b'@var{binary-digits}'} format.
Examples: @code{b'1010'}, @code{b'11111111'}
@item Word/Dword (String)
@code{w'@var{data}'} or @code{w"@var{data}"} format.
Used for assembler word data.
Examples: @code{w'ABCD'}, @code{w"test"}
@end table
All numeric formats are internally converted to their decimal integer value
for AST representation.

@item String literals
Text enclosed in double quotes (@code{"..."}). 
Examples: @code{"Hello, world!"}, @code{"player_name"}
@end table

@subsection Identifiers

Identifiers consist of letters, digits, underscores (@code{_}), and hyphens (@code{-}).
They must begin with a letter or underscore.
Identifiers are case-insensitive and normalized to @code{Header.Case} format in the AST.

@subsubsection Identifier Normalization

All identifiers are automatically normalized to the @code{Header.Case} format with dots 
as segment separators:

@table @asis
@item Input format
Identifiers with hyphens or underscores as separators (e.g., @code{player-name}, @code{player_name}).
@item Output format (AST)
Header.Case with dots (e.g., @code{Player.Name}).
@end table

@table @asis
@item Example 1
@code{player_score} → @code{Player.Score}
@item Example 2
@code{get-item-count} → @code{Get.Item.Count}
@item Example 3
@code{my_var_name} → @code{My.Var.Name}
@item Example 4
@code{X} → @code{X} (unchanged if no separators)
@end table

This normalization ensures consistent, language-appropriate naming conventions in the generated AST.

@section Statements

An AGI program is a sequence of statements, each executed in order unless modified by
control flow statements.

@subsection Conditional Statement: IF-THEN-ELSE

@defsyn
IF @var{condition} THEN @var{true-statement} [ELSE @var{false-statement}]
@end defsyn

Executes @var{true-statement} if @var{condition} evaluates to true (non-zero);
otherwise, executes @var{false-statement} if present.
The @var{condition} is an expression (see @ref{Expressions}).

@example
IF (score > 100) THEN
    SET difficulty 1
ELSE
    SET difficulty 0
END-IF
@end example

Note: The @code{END-IF} keyword is not part of the language syntax; it is used in examples
for clarity but is not required by the parser. The parser accepts a single statement after
THEN and ELSE; to create a block, use multiple statements in sequence (though the current
parser only allows one statement per branch—see @ref{Limitations}).

@subsection Subroutine Call: GOSUB

@defsyn
GOSUB @var{routine-name}
@end defsyn

Calls the subroutine named @var{routine-name}. Execution continues after the GOSUB
statement when a RETURN is encountered in the subroutine.

@example
GOSUB InitializeGame
@end example

@subsection Unconditional Jump: GOTO

@defsyn
GOTO @var{label}
@end defsyn

Transfers control to the statement labeled @var{label}. Labels are identifiers.

@example
GOTO StartLoop
@end example

@subsection Subroutine Return: RETURN

@defsyn
RETURN
@end defsyn

Returns from the current subroutine to the statement following the most recent GOSUB.

@example
RETURN
@end example

@subsection Assignment: @var{variable} = @var{expression}

@defsyn
@var{variable} = @var{expression}
@end defsyn

Evaluates @var{expression} and stores the result in @var{variable}.

@example
player_score = 0
x = (y + 10)
@end example

@subsection Increment: INCREMENT @var{variable}

@defsyn
INCREMENT @var{variable}
@end defsyn

Adds 1 to @var{variable}.

@example
INCREMENT lives
@end example

@subsection Decrement: DECREMENT @var{variable}

@defsyn
DECREMENT @var{variable}
@end defsyn

Subtracts 1 from @var{variable}.

@example
DECREMENT lives
@end example

@subsection Set to One: SET @var{variable}

@defsyn
SET @var{variable}
@end defsyn

Sets @var{variable} to 1.

@example
SET flag
@end example

@subsection Reset to Zero: RESET @var{variable}

@defsyn
RESET @var{variable}
@end defsyn

Sets @var{variable} to 0.

@example
RESET flag
@end example

@section Expressions

Expressions compute values and can consist of literals, identifiers, operators, and
parentheses for grouping.

@subsection Arithmetic Expressions

Arithmetic expressions use the operators @code{+}, @code{-}, @code{*}, @code{/},
@code{<<}, and @code{>>} to combine numeric values.

@example
score + 10
(x * y) / 2
value << 2
@end example

@subsection Comparison Expressions

Comparison expressions evaluate to 1 (true) or 0 (false) and use the operators
@code{=}, @code{<}, @code{>}, @code{<=}, @code{>=}, @code{<>}.

@example
(score = 100)
(x < y)
(flag <> 0)
@end example

@subsection Logical Expressions

Logical expressions combine boolean values using @code{AND} and @code{OR}.
The @code{NOT} operator negates a boolean expression.

@example
(score > 0) AND (lives > 0)
(NOT flag)
(score < 10) OR (time > 100)
@end example

@subsection Operator Precedence

Operators have the following precedence (highest first):
@enumerate
@item Parentheses @code{( @var{expression} @code{)}}
@item Multiplication, division, and shifts: @code{*}, @code{/}, @code{<<}, @code{>>}
@item Addition and subtraction: @code{+}, @code{-}
@item Comparisons: @code{=}, @code{<}, @code{>}, @code{<=}, @code{>=}, @code{<>}
@item Logical NOT: @code{NOT}
@item Logical AND: @code{AND}
@item Logical OR: @code{OR}
@end enumerate

@subsection Primary Expressions

Primary expressions are:
@table @asis
@item Numeric literals: @code{42}, @code{0}, @code{x'FF'}, @code{o'77'}, @code{b'1010'}
@item String literals: @code{"Hello, world!"}
@item Identifiers: @code{score}, @code{player_x}, @code{Player.Score}
@end table

@subsection Numeric Literal Examples

Examples of numeric literals using different formats:
@table @asis
@item Decimal
@code{100}, @code{d'255'} (both equivalent; evaluates to 100 or 255)
@item Hexadecimal
@code{x'FF'} (255 in decimal), @code{x'1A'} (26 in decimal), @code{x'100'} (256 in decimal)
@item Octal
@code{o'77'} (63 in decimal), @code{o'10'} (8 in decimal), @code{o'777'} (511 in decimal)
@item Binary
@code{b'1010'} (10 in decimal), @code{b'11111111'} (255 in decimal), @code{b'1'} (1 in decimal)
@item Word/Dword (String)
@code{w'ABCD'} (string for assembler), @code{w"test"} (string for assembler)
@end table

All numeric formats are converted to their decimal integer equivalents when parsed
and stored in the AST. This allows the rest of the compiler to work uniformly with
integers regardless of the input representation.

@section Limitations

The current AGI frontend implementation has the following limitations:

@itemize @bullet
@item Only one statement is allowed in the THEN and ELSE branches of an IF statement.
  To execute multiple statements, use subroutines (GOSUB/RETURN).
@item The keywords NEWROOM, LOADLOGICS, QUIT, ASSIGN, SAID, TEST, POSN, CONTROLLER,
  HAVEKEY, and the operator @code{.} are reserved for future use and have no effect.
@item String literals are supported but cannot be used in expressions (they are only
  allowed as literals; no string operations are defined).
@end itemize

@section Example Program

@example
REM This program demonstrates basic AGI frontend constructs
SET score 0
SET lives 3

MAIN_LOOP:
IF (lives = 0) THEN
    GOTO GAME_OVER
ELSE
    INCREMENT score
END-IF

IF (score > 1000) THEN
    SET lives (lives - 1)
    SET score 0
END-IF

GOTO MAIN_LOOP

GAME_OVER:
RETURN
@end example
